# Castilian Spanish letter-to-sound.
#
# One block a letter and its arms in order, the first that matches winning.
# The last arm of a block is the bare letter: what it says when nothing else
# applies. An arm may also say what must stand to its left and right without
# swallowing it -- `after', `before', and `vowel', `consonant' or `glide' in
# either place -- and `at start' or `at end' for a run that has to begin or
# end where the piece of the word does.
#
# Spanish spells what it says, so most of these are one arm. The letters with
# real rules in them are c, g, l, n, p, q, r, s, x, y, z and the vowels, and
# those are not written here yet; h is not either, because it is silent and
# `says nothing' is not ready -- tools/rules/letters.py says why.

rules in st_phone.obj named apply_span_%s_rules

letter b
  b    says b

letter d
  d    says d

letter f
  f    says f

letter j
  j    says j

letter k
  k    says k

letter m
  m    says m

letter ñ as enye
  ñ    says N

letter v
  v    says b         # v and b are one sound

letter w
  w    says w

letter z
  z    says T         # the Castilian theta

letter ç as c_cedilla
  ç    says s

letter l
  ll   says L         # calle, llamar
  l    says l

letter q
  qu before e   says k    # que, quedar
  qu before i   says k    # quien, quinto
  qu before é   says k
  qu before í   says k
  q             says k

letter t
  t    says t

letter s
  s before d   says z     # desde
  s before l   says z     # isla
  s before m   says z     # abismo
  s before n   says z     # asno
  s            says s
